From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Franck Nijhof <opensource@frenck.dev>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 16:27:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Patch keep log files out of the config share

Upstream writes every log next to the user's data, which for this
add-on means the /config share that Home Assistant backs up and that
lives on the same partition as everything else. A busy proxy can grow
those files without bound.

Two destinations replace it:

  - The per-host access and error logs, plus the backend log, move to
    /var/log/nginx-proxy-manager. That is the container's own writable
    layer, so it never reaches a backup and every add-on update starts
    it empty. The Logs viewer added upstream in v2.16.0 reads these
    paths, so it keeps working.

  - The global and fallback logs, which no part of the UI reads, go to
    the container's stdout and show up in the add-on log.

The logrotate rules that ship with these files switch from a weekly
schedule to a size cap, since the app runs logrotate on a timer rather
than from cron and a weekly rule would almost never fire.

Certbot keeps logging to /config/logs. Those logs are small, capped by
max-log-backups in letsencrypt.ini, and worth keeping across updates
when a certificate fails to issue.

Covers the split of conf.d/include/log.conf into log-proxy.conf and
log-stream.conf introduced upstream in v2.13.0.
